5.3 31aug2026
* OWM forecasts now record probability of precipitation and the daily minimum
  and maximum temperature.  OpenWeatherMap sends `pop`, `main.temp_min` and
  `main.temp_max` in every period and the parser had been discarding all
  three; they now populate the pop, tempMin and tempMax columns, so
  $forecast.weather_summary('OWM') reports them like any other source.  Found
  by sbsrouteur, who reported it in pull request #1 in January 2021.

  Visibility, which the same report proposed putting in obvis, is deliberately
  not included: obvis is a three-character code for an obstruction to
  visibility (fog, haze, blowing snow), not a distance, and OWM's `visibility`
  is metres of visual range.  Mapping one to the other would take a threshold
  table that the service does not supply.

5.2 31aug2026
* The installer now writes a commented configuration stanza.  The [Forecast],
  [DataBindings], [Databases] and [StdReport] sections it merges into a fresh
  weewx.conf arrive with comments explaining every option: what each of the
  nine sources needs, where to get its API key, that the tide location is an
  xtide location name, and that the services you do not use should be removed
  from archive_services.  Previously those sections landed as bare key = value
  lines, ten of them INSERT_..._HERE placeholders with nothing anywhere
  saying what to put there.  No option changed its value or its meaning; this
  affects fresh installs only, since the installer never rewrites a weewx.conf
  that already has these sections.

* Tide and UK Met Office forecasts now check the Python version at startup.
  Both need Python 3.7: tide generation calls subprocess.run with
  capture_output, and the UKMO parser calls datetime.fromisoformat.  On an
  older Python, tides failed with a TypeError partway through generating a
  forecast, and UKMO downloaded successfully and then died with an
  AttributeError in every parse.  Each source now logs that it requires
  Python 3.7 or later and declines to run, the same way they already do when
  a location or an API key is missing.  The other seven sources are
  unaffected and run as before.  The requirement is not new, it was simply
  never stated nor checked.  The README and the manual now state it.

  The tide check sits in both the service and XTideForecast.generate, so the
  command-line harness reports the version too rather than raising TypeError.

* Fix two invalid CSS properties in the iconic skin.  forecast_iconic.css
  declared "webkit-radius" and "moz-radius", which are not properties -- the
  vendor-prefixed spellings need a leading hyphen -- so both lines had never
  done anything, and the "border-radius" beside them was already doing the
  work.  Rendering is unchanged.  The Nu validator rejects them, but only
  when it is pointed at the css: a validation run that globs *.html never
  reads an external stylesheet, which is why these survived.
  Also removed a duplicated forecast_strip.css from the skin's copy_once
  list, which had been copying that one file twice.

* Fix the command-line UKMO test harness, which could not run at all.  UKMO
  is the one source keyed by latitude and longitude rather than a location
  name, but --method=ukmo passed --loc where the code wanted latitude, and
  omitted longitude entirely; the parse path passed a location keyword that
  UKMOForecast.parse does not accept.  Both raised TypeError.  There are now
  --latitude and --longitude options, and both UKMO paths use them.  Only the
  command-line debugging tool was affected; UKMO forecasts running under
  weewxd were never on this path.

* Cut forecast.py's module docstring from 506 lines to 44.  It had carried a
  full copy of the user reference -- the configuration stanza, the skin
  options, every label, the template tags -- which the manual now covers, and
  the two copies had already drifted (the Zambretti interval default was wrong
  in the docstring for years).  What remains is what a reader of the code
  needs: which service version each parser was written against, the two-part
  design, why one table holds every method with unused fields left NULL, the
  threading model, and the two sources that require Python 3.7.  install.py's
  comments now point at the manual rather than at the docstring, so the text
  the installer writes into weewx.conf sends users somewhere searchable.

* Rework the README around the manual.  It had grown to 276 lines that
  duplicated what the manual now covers -- the full configuration stanza, the
  xtide build, the comparison report -- with the manual itself mentioned once,
  below the copyright block.  It is now 139 lines: what the extension is, a
  table of the nine sources, what you get, and the install commands, with
  links into the manual for the detail.  Nothing was dropped; it moved.

* Add a manual, published with GitHub Pages at
  https://chaunceygardiner.github.io/weewx-forecast/.  Installation, every
  option of all nine sources and where to get each API key, the $forecast
  template tags, the full set of translatable labels, the comparison report
  and troubleshooting.  Most of this reference existed only in a 500-line
  comment at the top of forecast.py, where no user would find it.

* Fix two documentation errors found while writing the manual: the README's
  UK Met Office example spelled the option "longtitude", which the code does
  not read (it reads "longitude").  A user copying that example lost BOTH
  coordinates, not just the misspelled one: UKMO falls back to the station's
  latitude and longitude when either is missing, so the correctly spelled
  latitude was silently discarded too.  Also, forecast.py's configuration
  comments gave the Zambretti interval default as 3600 when the code uses 600.

* The weewx version check compares the major version numerically.  It was a
  string comparison, which reads "10.0.0" as less than "4", so the extension
  would have refused to load under weewx 10 and later with "WeeWX 4 is
  required".  No released weewx is affected; this is a latent failure being
  fixed before it can bite.

5.1
* Honor the shutdown flag that weewx 5.5 hands to report generators.  The
  compare skin's ForecastPlotGenerator now checks it while scanning the
  forecast database and while collecting data, and passes it on to weewx's
  image generator, which checks it between plots.  Shutting weewxd down in
  the middle of a report no longer waits for every comparison plot to be
  drawn.  Earlier weewx releases have no such flag and are unaffected.

* Fix weewxd being unable to shut down during a forecast update when
  `single_thread = True`.  weewxd stops by raising Terminate from its SIGTERM
  handler, inside whatever the main thread is running; do_forecast's broad
  exception handler swallowed it as a forecast failure.  Terminate is now
  handed back.  The threaded default (single_thread = False) was never
  affected -- signals only reach the main thread.

* Fix the error path in do_forecast: a failure in get_forecast (a download
  error, say) raised UnboundLocalError from the handler itself -- it reports
  dbm_dict, which is not assigned until after the forecast is fetched -- so
  the real error was lost and the exception escaped instead of being logged.

* Fix the compare report failing whenever there is no forecast data to plot:
  a source that has not been downloaded yet, a misspelled source, or an
  issued_since that excludes every issue.  The image generator was handed a
  zero or negative time span and threw "scaletime called with tmax <= tmin"
  for every plot, once per report cycle.  The generator now logs that it has
  nothing to plot and stops.

* The comparison generator's scratch database now lives beside the station's
  own databases (SQLITE_ROOT, e.g. /home/weewx/archive/fpg.sdb) instead of at
  the fixed /var/tmp/fpg.sdb.  It is deleted and recreated on every run, so
  two weewx instances on one machine used to fight over the one file; now
  each has its own.  It also no longer sits in a sticky, world-writable
  directory, where a file left behind by another user could not be removed.
  Nothing to configure.  A MySQL-only station has no SQLITE_ROOT; it gets a
  pid-stamped file in the system temp directory instead (WEEWX_ROOT is no
  place to write -- on a package install it is /etc/weewx).

* The comparison generator's tests (test_cmp.py) now build a forecast
  database with real data and check that the expected plots come out.  They
  ran against an empty database before, so every one of them passed while
  generating nothing.

5.0.1
* Fix the compare skin's obsolete doctype: index.html.tmpl declared an XHTML
  1.0 Strict doctype, which HTML validators flag as obsolete.  It now uses the
  HTML5 `<!DOCTYPE html>` (with `<html lang="en">`), matching the forecast skin.

* Remove trailing slashes from void elements (`<br>`, `<img>`, `<link>`)
  throughout the forecast and compare skins.  Under the HTML5 doctype the Nu
  Html Checker flagged every self-closed void element ("Trailing slash on void
  elements has no effect and interacts badly with unquoted attribute values"),
  producing thousands of validator messages across the generated pages.  This
  is purely a markup cleanup; the rendered output is unchanged.

5.0
* Dark Sky shut down its API.  The DS forecast source now points at Pirate
  Weather (https://pirateweather.net), a free, open drop-in that speaks the
  same protocol.  No weewx.conf restructuring is required: the [[DS]] section,
  the DSForecast service, and the 'DS' method tag are all unchanged.  Dark Sky
  users just replace their api_key with a Pirate Weather api_key (get one at
  https://pirate-weather.apiable.io/).  (The DS name is kept internally to
  avoid breaking existing service lists; the source is Pirate Weather.)

* Fix XTide event timestamps for a tide station in a different timezone than
  the weewx server.  tide is now run in UTC (-z) and parsed as UTC, so
  event times are correct regardless of the station's zone.

* Fix XTide error handling and robustness: a failing `tide` invocation crashed
  (joining bytes with str) instead of logging the error; the return-code check
  never fired (it read returncode before the process had exited) and the child
  was never reaped.  The command is now built without a shell (a location
  containing quotes can no longer break or inject into it) and has a timeout.

* Fix UKMO wind speed, which was reported about 3.6x too low: the m/s wind
  field was converted to mph with the km/h factor.

* Fix a crash (NameError) in the Aeris parser when the service returns an
  error response with success=false and error=null.

* Fix crashes on present-but-null fields in the OWM, UKMO, WWO and Pirate
  Weather (formerly Dark Sky) parsers.

* Fix Pirate Weather precipitation.  precipAccumulation is the TOTAL
  precipitation for the period, in inches under us units -- not snowfall in cm.
  It was recorded as snow (qsf) with the wrong (cm) unit conversion, and rain
  quantity (qpf) was never recorded at all (e.g. a rainy day reported snow).
  It is now routed to qpf or qsf by precipType, in inches.

* Fix the NE compass boundary in deg2dir: bearings just above 65.5 degrees
  were labeled E instead of NE (the boundary is 67.5 degrees).

* Fix a NWS parse crash (KeyError) when a point-forecast block contains only
  3-hourly or only 6-hourly rows.

* Fix the daily weather summary: a period falling exactly on local midnight was
  counted in two days (inflating qpf/qsf totals), and periods near midnight
  were mis-grouped by an hour on daylight-saving-transition days.

* Close the HTTP response in every download path (previously the socket
  leaked), and do not sleep after the final failed save/prune retry.

* Remove Python 2 remnants (the cjson/simplejson import fallback and the
  related dead code); the extension is Python 3 only.

* Fix several bugs that kept the compare skin's comparison report from working:
  - ForecastPlotGenerator wrote its plot images to the top-level public_html
    instead of the report's own HTML_ROOT (e.g. public_html/compare).
  - the skin never rendered its index.html page because its generator list
    omitted CheetahGenerator; it is now included.
  - the plot fonts referenced the obsolete /usr/share/fonts/truetype/ttf-dejavu
    path, so plots failed on current systems; now /usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu.
  - a single-source plot (e.g. 'source = NWS') was iterated character by
    character ('N', 'W', 'S') because a str passes the "is it iterable?" guard,
    so the per-source comparison plots never found any data; a single source is
    now wrapped in a list.
  The compare skin also gains a Pirate Weather column.

* The automated test suite now runs green: parser tests against freshly
  captured responses (Pirate Weather, Weather Underground, UK Met Office, Open
  Weather Map, Aeris), XTide against the real tide program, and the compare
  skin's generator.  Pirate Weather gains its first tests.

4.2
* Handle change to how events are formatted in xtide 2.16

  Location in xtide 2.15 and earlier substitute '|' for , in location:
      Palo Alto Yacht Harbor| San Francisco Bay| California,2024-07-07,1:12 AM PDT,8.50 ft,High Tide
  In xtide 2.16, location contains commas and the field is enclosed in quotes:
      "Palo Alto Yacht Harbor, San Francisco Bay, California",2024-07-07,1:12 AM PDT,8.50 ft,"High Tide"

* Convert readme.txt to markdown (README.md); update its xtide build
  instructions to install xtide 2.16 and the latest harmonics data.

